saveOutputs: false
cells:
  - kind: 1
    value: "# 11f · Step 5 – Who can help, and where? (SQL property graph and spatial)\n\nThe knowledge graph says: probably a ruptured anterior cruciate ligament, MRI within 48 hours. Jonas' talk is tomorrow afternoon. He needs a specialist today and an MRI before the talk – in Nuremberg, where he lives and where the conference takes place.\n\n**Question:** Which path through the care network leads from Jonas' GP to an MRI before his talk – in Nuremberg or Fürth, ordered by drive time?\n\n- **Features:** SQL Property Graph (`GRAPH_TABLE`, `MATCH`, path patterns, quantifiers, `VERTEX_ID`/`EDGE_ID`), Spatial (`SDO_ANYINTERACT` with OSM city boundaries), routing (`SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE`)\n- **Pattern:** M2 – walk the network, weight by location\n- **Data:** property graph `CARE_NETWORK` on `PATIENTS`, `PROVIDERS`, `DEVICES`, `APPOINTMENT_SLOTS`, `PATIENT_PROVIDER_RELATIONS`, `PROVIDER_RELATIONS`; `OSM_BOUNDARIES` (city boundaries of Nuremberg and Fürth from OpenStreetMap); `PARKING_LOTS`\n- **Run:** as the demo user (DOAG2026) in SQL Developer for VS Code, after `10_create_property_graph.sql`. Run the cells from top to bottom.\n\nTime frame of the story: the search starts on Monday, 16 November 2026, at noon (`TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'`), Jonas' talk is on Tuesday afternoon. The MRI has to take place before Tuesday noon (`TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'`).\n\n> Fictitious demo: no medical device and no medical advice. Places from OpenStreetMap (© OpenStreetMap contributors, ODbL); all persons and story places are synthetic."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Session settings\n\nThe story takes place on Monday, 16 November 2026, in Franconia. With the session time zone `Europe/Berlin`, all timestamps and literals read in local time.\n\nThe SQLcl settings in the second cell make the text output readable: columns as wide as their content instead of their declared length (`SQLFORMAT ansiconsole`; values from JSON are `VARCHAR2(4000)`), one header per result (`PAGESIZE`), wider lines (`LINESIZE`) and complete JSON documents and CLOBs (`LONG`, `LONGCHUNKSIZE`)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "ALTER SESSION SET TIME_ZONE = 'Europe/Berlin';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SET SQLFORMAT ansiconsole\nSET PAGESIZE 1000\nSET LINESIZE 250\nSET LONG 1000000\nSET LONGCHUNKSIZE 250"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 1. The graph\n\nPatients, practices, devices and appointments live in relational tables. The property graph is a view of these tables as vertices and edges.\n\n**Question:** What does the care network look like as a graph?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`CARE_NETWORK` is defined on the relational tables; no data is copied. Vertices: `patient`, `provider`, `device`, `slot`. Edges: `care_relation` (patient → provider), `provider_relation` (provider → provider: *ueberweist_an* = refers to, *kooperiert_mit* = cooperates with), `operates` (provider → device), `offers` (provider → slot).\n\n**Result:** the graph is in `TRUSTED` mode and shows exactly the rows of its tables: 25 patients, 28 providers, 10 devices, 435 appointment slots, 27 patient relations and 23 relations between providers. `operates` and `offers` are not counted separately – each device and each slot is one edge from its provider. A graph view on existing tables: nothing is copied, nothing has to be synchronized."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT graph_name, graph_mode\nFROM   user_property_graphs\nWHERE  graph_name = 'CARE_NETWORK';"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT 'patient' AS label, COUNT(*) AS elements\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH (v IS patient)  COLUMNS (v.patient_id  AS id))\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'provider', COUNT(*)\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH (v IS provider) COLUMNS (v.provider_id AS id))\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'device', COUNT(*)\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH (v IS device)   COLUMNS (v.device_id   AS id))\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'slot', COUNT(*)\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH (v IS slot)     COLUMNS (v.slot_id     AS id))\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'care_relation', COUNT(*)\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH () -[e IS care_relation]-> ()     COLUMNS (e.relation_id AS id))\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'provider_relation', COUNT(*)\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network MATCH () -[e IS provider_relation]-> () COLUMNS (e.relation_id AS id));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 2. Jonas' direct relations\n\nEvery path starts with Jonas.\n\n**Question:** Which practices is Jonas connected to?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The simplest path pattern: one edge from the patient to a provider. `MATCH (p IS patient WHERE …) -[r IS care_relation]-> (pr IS provider)` reads like a picture of the graph; `COLUMNS` defines the result columns.\n\n**Result:** GP PR01 (*hat_hausarzt*); in 2021 he was treated by the sports medicine practice PR30 and the physiotherapy PR50 (*war_in_behandlung*)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT *\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001') -[r IS care_relation]-> (pr IS provider)\n         COLUMNS (p.first_name     AS patient,\n                  r.relation_type,\n                  r.since,\n                  pr.provider_id,\n                  pr.name          AS provider,\n                  pr.specialty,\n                  pr.city))\nORDER  BY since;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 3. Referral paths to an MRI device\n\nA ruptured cruciate ligament needs an MRI, and in Germany the way there leads through referrals.\n\n**Question:** Which referral paths lead from Jonas' GP to an MRI device?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A path over four edges: GP → refers to a specialist → who cooperates with a radiology → that operates an MRI device. Conditions on edges and vertices stand directly in the pattern.\n\n**Result:** two paths: via the orthopaedics *Orthopädie am Stadtpark* (PR10) to *Radiologie Nürnberg Nord* (PR20, 3 tesla), and via the sports medicine practice PR30 to *MRT-Zentrum Südstadt* (PR21, 1.5 tesla, open design)."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT *\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n               -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->   (gp   IS provider)\n               -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']->  (spec IS provider)\n               -[r3 IS provider_relation WHERE r3.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad  IS provider)\n               -[o  IS operates]-> (d IS device WHERE d.modality = 'MRT')\n         COLUMNS (gp.name            AS gp,\n                  spec.provider_id   AS specialist_id,\n                  spec.name          AS specialist,\n                  rad.provider_id    AS radiology_id,\n                  rad.name           AS radiology,\n                  d.device_id        AS mri,\n                  d.field_strength_t AS tesla,\n                  d.design))\nORDER  BY specialist_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 4. Why not the obvious way?\n\nJonas knows the sports physician from 2021 – the obvious choice.\n\n**Question:** Which specialist has a free appointment before the talk?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Jonas knows the sports medicine practice PR30 from 2021, so that would be his first choice. The query collects the free consultation slots of every specialist the GP refers to and groups them: earliest free slot and whether there is one before the talk.\n\n`EXISTS` on `PATIENT_PROVIDER_RELATIONS` marks the practices Jonas already knows.\n\n**Result:** the sports medicine practice PR30, which Jonas knows, has its first free slot on Wednesday, 18.11., at 11:00 – after his talk. The orthopaedics PR10, unknown to him, has exactly one free slot before the talk: today, Monday, at 14:00. The familiar way is the slow one; the graph finds the fast one."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT g.specialist_id,\n       g.specialist,\n       CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1\n                         FROM   patient_provider_relations r\n                         WHERE  r.patient_id  = 'P0001'\n                         AND    r.provider_id = g.specialist_id\n                         AND    r.relation_type = 'war_in_behandlung')\n            THEN 'yes' ELSE 'no' END                                   AS known_to_jonas,\n       TO_CHAR(MIN(g.slot_start), 'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI',\n               'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN')                            AS earliest_free_slot,\n       COUNT(CASE WHEN g.slot_start < TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin' THEN 1 END)               AS free_slots_before_talk\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n               -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->  (gp IS provider)\n               -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']-> (spec IS provider)\n               -[o  IS offers]-> (s IS slot WHERE s.status = 'frei' AND s.slot_type = 'Sprechstunde')\n         WHERE s.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n         COLUMNS (spec.provider_id AS specialist_id,\n                  spec.name        AS specialist,\n                  s.slot_start)) g\nGROUP  BY g.specialist_id, g.specialist\nORDER  BY MIN(g.slot_start);"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 5. The path with two appointments\n\nTwo appointments have to fit together: the consultation first, the MRI afterwards, both before Tuesday noon.\n\n**Question:** Which combination of consultation and MRI works?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Two path patterns in one `MATCH`, separated by a comma, share the variables `spec` and `rad` (join semantics): the referral path, plus a free consultation slot at the specialist and a free knee MRI slot at the radiology. The `WHERE` after the patterns compares the two slots: the MRI comes after the consultation and before the talk.\n\n**Result:** *Orthopädie am Stadtpark* today at 14:00 → *Radiologie Nürnberg Nord*, MRI tomorrow at 07:30 on device MRT01."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT specialist,\n       TO_CHAR(consultation, 'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN') AS consultation,\n       radiology,\n       mri_device,\n       TO_CHAR(mri_slot,     'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN') AS mri\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n               -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->   (gp   IS provider)\n               -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']->  (spec IS provider)\n               -[r3 IS provider_relation WHERE r3.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad  IS provider),\n               (spec) -[o1 IS offers]-> (s1 IS slot WHERE s1.status = 'frei' AND s1.slot_type = 'Sprechstunde'),\n               (rad)  -[o2 IS offers]-> (s2 IS slot WHERE s2.status = 'frei' AND s2.slot_type = 'MRT Knie')\n         WHERE s1.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n           AND s2.slot_start >  s1.slot_start\n           AND s2.slot_start <  TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n         COLUMNS (spec.name     AS specialist,\n                  s1.slot_start AS consultation,\n                  rad.name      AS radiology,\n                  s2.device_id  AS mri_device,\n                  s2.slot_start AS mri_slot))\nORDER  BY consultation, mri_slot\nFETCH  FIRST 5 ROWS ONLY;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### The same path for the graph visualization\n\n**Question:** How do we show this path as a picture of the graph?\n\n**How the query answers it:**\n\n`VERTEX_ID` and `EDGE_ID` return the identifiers of the matched vertices and edges as JSON. SQL Developer for VS Code can show such a result as a graph: patient, GP, specialist, radiology and the two slots.\n\n**Result:** one row with 6 vertices and 5 edges, each identified by graph, table and key: Jonas (P0001) –PP001→ GP PR01 –RE001→ orthopaedics PR10 –RE009→ radiology PR20; PR10 offers slot SL0046 (consultation), PR20 offers slot SL0088 (MRI). The `OFFERS` edges carry the key of the slot itself – the edge and its target vertex are the same row."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT *\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n               -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->   (gp   IS provider)\n               -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']->  (spec IS provider)\n               -[r3 IS provider_relation WHERE r3.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad  IS provider),\n               (spec) -[o1 IS offers]-> (s1 IS slot WHERE s1.status = 'frei' AND s1.slot_type = 'Sprechstunde'),\n               (rad)  -[o2 IS offers]-> (s2 IS slot WHERE s2.status = 'frei' AND s2.slot_type = 'MRT Knie')\n         WHERE s1.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n           AND s2.slot_start >  s1.slot_start\n           AND s2.slot_start <  TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n         COLUMNS (VERTEX_ID(p)    AS patient_vertex,\n                  EDGE_ID(r1)     AS gp_edge,\n                  VERTEX_ID(gp)   AS gp_vertex,\n                  EDGE_ID(r2)     AS referral_edge,\n                  VERTEX_ID(spec) AS specialist_vertex,\n                  EDGE_ID(r3)     AS cooperation_edge,\n                  VERTEX_ID(rad)  AS radiology_vertex,\n                  EDGE_ID(o1)     AS consultation_edge,\n                  VERTEX_ID(s1)   AS consultation_vertex,\n                  EDGE_ID(o2)     AS mri_edge,\n                  VERTEX_ID(s2)   AS mri_vertex));"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 6. Variable-length paths: who is reachable from Jonas?\n\nBeyond the direct referral there may be more options.\n\n**Question:** Which practices can Jonas reach within two steps?\n\n**How the query answers it:** A quantifier on the edge `-[e IS provider_relation]->{1,2}` follows one or two referral or cooperation edges. The query lists all providers reachable within two hops from the practices Jonas is connected to (GP and former practices). The variable `e` stands for one or two edges here.\n\n**Result:** five providers, all in Nuremberg: the two specialist practices PR10 and PR30, the physiotherapy PR50 and the two radiologies with MRI, PR20 and PR21. Jonas' network is small – which is why one free slot at PR10 decides the day. `GROUP BY` lists each provider once, even if it can be reached on more than one path."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT provider_id,\n       name,\n       provider_type,\n       city\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n               -[r IS care_relation]-> (start_pr IS provider)\n               -[e IS provider_relation]->{1,2} (x IS provider)\n         COLUMNS (x.provider_id, x.name, x.provider_type, x.city))\nGROUP  BY provider_id, name, provider_type, city\nORDER  BY provider_type, provider_id;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 7. Graph and geography: the location in the care network\n\nThe graph knows relations, not places. Jonas lives in Nuremberg and gives his talk there.\n\n**Question:** Which specialist–radiology pairs lie inside Nuremberg or Fürth?\n\n**How the query answers it:** The graph does not know where the practices are; `PROVIDERS.GEOM` does. The query lists all pairs specialist → cooperating radiology with an MRI device in the whole region and checks with `SDO_ANYINTERACT` whether **both** practices lie inside the OpenStreetMap city boundaries of Nuremberg or Fürth. Jonas lives in Nuremberg and gives his talk there.\n\nThe graph columns are joined with `PROVIDERS` by key; the spatial operator runs in an `EXISTS` subquery against `OSM_BOUNDARIES`.\n\n**Result:** the pairs in Erlangen, Forchheim and Bayreuth drop out; three pairs in Nuremberg remain, two of them lead to the *MRT-Zentrum Südstadt*."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "SELECT g.specialist,\n       sp.city                                                         AS specialist_city,\n       g.radiology,\n       rp.city                                                         AS radiology_city,\n       g.mri_device,\n       CASE WHEN EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM osm_boundaries b\n                         WHERE  b.name IN ('Nürnberg', 'Fürth')\n                         AND    SDO_ANYINTERACT(sp.geom, b.geom) = 'TRUE')\n             AND EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM osm_boundaries b\n                         WHERE  b.name IN ('Nürnberg', 'Fürth')\n                         AND    SDO_ANYINTERACT(rp.geom, b.geom) = 'TRUE')\n            THEN 'inside' ELSE 'outside' END                           AS nuremberg_or_fuerth\nFROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n         MATCH (spec IS provider WHERE spec.provider_type = 'Facharztpraxis')\n               -[r IS provider_relation WHERE r.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad IS provider)\n               -[o IS operates]-> (d IS device WHERE d.modality = 'MRT')\n         COLUMNS (spec.provider_id AS specialist_id,\n                  spec.name        AS specialist,\n                  rad.provider_id  AS radiology_id,\n                  rad.name         AS radiology,\n                  d.device_id      AS mri_device)) g\nJOIN   providers sp ON sp.provider_id = g.specialist_id\nJOIN   providers rp ON rp.provider_id = g.radiology_id\nORDER  BY nuremberg_or_fuerth, specialist_city, g.specialist;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "## 8. Pattern M2 in one query: path, area, drive time\n\nNow everything together: the path, the area and the drive from where Jonas will be picked up.\n\n**Question:** Which option is best when the drive time counts too?\n\n**How the query answers it:** Now everything together:\n\n1. **Graph:** referral path from Jonas' GP with a free consultation and a later free knee MRI before the talk (as in section 5).\n2. **Spatial:** both practices inside the city boundaries (`SDO_ANYINTERACT`).\n3. **Routing:** drive time from the pick-up point, the hiking car park *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite* (`PK03`), to the specialist with `SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE` (Oracle Maps service of the Autonomous AI Database; the answer is JSON, time in minutes).\n\nThe route is called once per candidate in the `MATERIALIZE`d CTE and then read twice with `JSON_VALUE`. The start coordinates come from scalar subqueries on `PARKING_LOTS`, so no join without a join condition is needed.\n\n**Result:** one candidate remains: *Orthopädie am Stadtpark* (PR10), consultation today at 14:00, MRI at *Radiologie Nürnberg Nord* tomorrow at 07:30 – 43 minutes and 64.5 km by car from the *Wanderparkplatz Hohe Leite*. Of the three pairs inside the city boundaries (section 7), only this one has both free slots before the talk. Graph, area and routing answer the question in one statement."
    languageId: markdown
  - kind: 2
    value: "WITH candidates AS (\n  SELECT g.*\n  FROM   GRAPH_TABLE (care_network\n           MATCH (p IS patient WHERE p.patient_id = 'P0001')\n                 -[r1 IS care_relation     WHERE r1.relation_type = 'hat_hausarzt']->   (gp   IS provider)\n                 -[r2 IS provider_relation WHERE r2.relation_type = 'ueberweist_an']->  (spec IS provider)\n                 -[r3 IS provider_relation WHERE r3.relation_type = 'kooperiert_mit']-> (rad  IS provider),\n                 (spec) -[o1 IS offers]-> (s1 IS slot WHERE s1.status = 'frei' AND s1.slot_type = 'Sprechstunde'),\n                 (rad)  -[o2 IS offers]-> (s2 IS slot WHERE s2.status = 'frei' AND s2.slot_type = 'MRT Knie')\n           WHERE s1.slot_start >= TIMESTAMP '2026-11-16 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n             AND s2.slot_start >  s1.slot_start\n             AND s2.slot_start <  TIMESTAMP '2026-11-17 12:00:00 Europe/Berlin'\n           COLUMNS (spec.provider_id AS specialist_id,\n                    spec.name        AS specialist,\n                    spec.lon         AS specialist_lon,\n                    spec.lat         AS specialist_lat,\n                    s1.slot_start    AS consultation,\n                    rad.provider_id  AS radiology_id,\n                    rad.name         AS radiology,\n                    s2.slot_start    AS mri_slot)) g\n  JOIN   providers sp ON sp.provider_id = g.specialist_id\n  JOIN   providers rp ON rp.provider_id = g.radiology_id\n  WHERE  EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM osm_boundaries b WHERE b.name IN ('Nürnberg', 'Fürth') AND SDO_ANYINTERACT(sp.geom, b.geom) = 'TRUE')\n  AND    EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM osm_boundaries b WHERE b.name IN ('Nürnberg', 'Fürth') AND SDO_ANYINTERACT(rp.geom, b.geom) = 'TRUE')\n),\nfirst_choice AS (\n  SELECT specialist_id, specialist, specialist_lon, specialist_lat,\n         MIN(consultation) AS consultation,\n         MIN(mri_slot) KEEP (DENSE_RANK FIRST ORDER BY consultation, mri_slot) AS mri_slot,\n         MIN(radiology)  KEEP (DENSE_RANK FIRST ORDER BY consultation, mri_slot) AS radiology\n  FROM   candidates\n  GROUP  BY specialist_id, specialist, specialist_lon, specialist_lat\n),\nroutes AS (\n  SELECT /*+ MATERIALIZE */\n         f.*,\n         SDO_GCDR.ELOC_ROUTE('fastest', 'km', 'minute',\n                             (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.x FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                             (SELECT pk.geom.sdo_point.y FROM parking_lots pk WHERE pk.parking_id = 'PK03'),\n                             f.specialist_lon, f.specialist_lat,\n                             'auto') AS route_json\n  FROM   first_choice f\n)\nSELECT specialist,\n       TO_CHAR(consultation, 'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN')       AS consultation,\n       radiology,\n       TO_CHAR(mri_slot,     'DY DD.MM. HH24:MI', 'NLS_DATE_LANGUAGE=GERMAN')       AS mri,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.time'     RETURNING NUMBER)) AS drive_min,\n       ROUND(JSON_VALUE(route_json, '$.routeResponse.route.distance' RETURNING NUMBER), 1) AS drive_km\nFROM   routes\nORDER  BY consultation, drive_min;"
    languageId: oracle-sql
  - kind: 1
    value: "### Summary\n\n- The property graph turns the relational care network into paths: GP → orthopaedics → radiology → free slots.\n- The obvious way via the sports medicine practice has no appointment in time.\n- Spatial keeps the candidates inside Nuremberg and Fürth, routing orders them by drive time (pattern M2).\n- Next: **11g** – how does Jonas get there?"
    languageId: markdown
